From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] soc_vpu: sleeping VPU-id wait, per-core bs_cnt, jz_nvpu error IRQ ends the wait

On top of 0098-0102.  Rebased onto 0097: jz_nvpu's vpu_wait_complete()
bail-out on a fatal signal is 0097's; this patch makes the wait killable
and adds the core reset before leaving.

 - soc_vpu_request(): when a specific vpu_id is requested and that VPU is
   busy, the old code gave the token back and busy-waited udelay(2000) up
   to FIND_VPU_TRY_TIME_THRESHOLD (100) times = 200 ms of CPU.  It now
   sleeps on a wait queue (free_vpu_list.vwq) that is woken whenever a
   VPU goes back to the free list, with the same 200 ms total limit
   (FIND_VPU_WAIT_MS), -EBUSY after it as before, -EINTR on a fatal
   signal.
 - helix: bs_cnt (BSFULL chunks of the running job) was a file-global,
   shared by all helix cores and never reset when a job was abandoned
   between two BSFULL waits (fatal signal, error, close), so the next
   job's ENDFLAG length check started from a stale count.  It is now a
   member of struct jz_vpu_helix and cleared in vpu_start().  The VPU is
   owned by one requester from START until the ENDFLAG wait releases it,
   so per-core state is exact; with CONFIG_VPU_HELIX_NUM=1 (T21/T23) it
   is otherwise identical.
 - jz_nvpu (T10/T20): the error interrupt (SHLD/TLB/BS/ACFG/TIMEOUT
   without ENDF) masked all sources but did not complete the wait, so
   vpu_wait_complete() always ran into the job timeout.  It now completes
   the wait and vpu_wait_complete() reports it as a failure (core reset,
   -1, as on timeout), like helix since 0102.  The wait is one killable
   wait instead of an interruptible one restarted with the full timeout
   on every signal; a fatal signal resets the core and returns -EINTR.
   vpu_start_vpu() drops a completion/status left by an earlier job
   before kicking the new one (helix: 0099).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
